\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The practical decision joins correctness and generated length. This is an explanatory diagram, not a new experiment.\relax }}{2}{figure.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces ON, OFF, and limits differ in how explicit notes precede the answer. LoRA can be combined with these policies; widths are schematic.\relax }}{6}{figure.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Conceptual LoRA pipeline. Training and the later thinking policy are separate choices.\relax }}{8}{figure.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Reporting pipeline. The document build reads historical outputs and grades; it performs no new model generation.\relax }}{11}{figure.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The main test collection has 164 HumanEval+, 166 LiveCodeBench easy, and 134 LiveCodeBench medium tasks.\relax }}{13}{figure.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The historical LoRA-2 funnel retains 157 examples from a 280-problem pool. It is separate from LoRA-1 and the full-set combinations.\relax }}{14}{figure.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Illustrative arithmetic showing equal problem weighting. These two example problems are not observations from the benchmark.\relax }}{17}{figure.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The saved uncertainty procedure resamples problems while retaining each paired comparison. Intervals are unadjusted.\relax }}{18}{figure.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Accuracy uses equal problem weights over all available attempts on 464 problems per method. Selected limits are 512, 1,024, and 2,048 by size.\relax }}{21}{figure.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces 0.8B benchmark breakdown, using all-attempt equal-problem accuracy. Group sizes are 164, 166, and 134. No hard contest tasks are included.\relax }}{23}{figure.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces 2B benchmark breakdown, using all-attempt equal-problem accuracy. Group sizes are 164, 166, and 134. No hard contest tasks are included.\relax }}{25}{figure.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces 4B benchmark breakdown, using all-attempt equal-problem accuracy. Group sizes are 164, 166, and 134. No hard contest tasks are included.\relax }}{27}{figure.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Paired first-attempt accuracy differences for each trained policy against the same policy without LoRA. Bars are unadjusted 95\% intervals; crossing zero indicates an inconclusive gain.\relax }}{29}{figure.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Mean total new tokens with equal problem weighting over available attempts. Thinking budgets do not cap the complete answer.\relax }}{30}{figure.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Each size has six points: 1 ON, 2 OFF, 3 selected limit, 4 LoRA alone, 5 LoRA + OFF, 6 LoRA + limit. All-attempt equal-problem summaries; overlapping points are retained. Lower tokens and higher accuracy are preferred.\relax }}{31}{figure.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Saved cap-hit rates across complete methods, using the combined problem-based summary. A cap hit is not a detected loop.\relax }}{32}{figure.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Original 234-problem study with 468 attempts per method. Cap percentages use all attempts; loop percentages use capped attempts only. These are different denominators.\relax }}{33}{figure.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Coverage is a denominator, not a performance score. Partial sweeps cannot establish a result on unmeasured problems.\relax }}{34}{figure.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Suggested follow-up workflow. It is a recommendation, not an additional completed evaluation.\relax }}{40}{figure.19}%
